#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:2
msgid "Build process overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:4
msgid ""
"Once a project has been added and a build is triggered, Read the Docs "
"executes a set of :term:`pre-defined jobs <pre-defined build jobs>` to "
"build and upload documentation. This page explains in detail what happens"
" behind the scenes, and includes an overview of how you can change this "
"process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:10
msgid "Understanding the build process"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:12
msgid ""
"Understanding how your content is built helps with debugging any problems"
" you might hit. It also gives you the knowledge to customize the build "
"process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:17
msgid ""
"All the steps are run inside a Docker container, using the image defined "
"in :ref:`config-file/v2:build.os`. The build has access to all :doc:`pre-"
"defined environment variables </reference/environment-variables>` and "
":doc:`custom environment variables </environment-variables>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:20
msgid "The build process includes the following jobs:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst
msgid "checkout"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:24
msgid ""
"Checks out a project's code from the Git repository. On |com_brand|, this"
" environment includes the SSH deploy key that gives access to the "
"repository."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst
msgid "system_dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:30
msgid ""
"Installs operating system and runtime dependencies. This includes "
"specific versions of a language (e.g. Python, Node.js, Go, Rust) and also"
" ``apt`` packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:33
msgid ""
":ref:`config-file/v2:build.tools` can be used to define a language "
"version, and :ref:`config-file/v2:build.apt_packages` to define ``apt`` "
"packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst
msgid "create_environment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:38
msgid ""
"Creates a Python environment to install all the dependencies in an "
"isolated and reproducible way. Depending on what's defined by the "
"project, a :term:`virtualenv` or a :ref:`conda environment <config-"
"file/v2:conda>` will be used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:44
#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:60
msgid ""
"This step is only executed if the :ref:`config-file/v2:sphinx` or :ref"
":`config-file/v2:mkdocs` keys are defined."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst
msgid "install"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:48
msgid ""
"Installs :doc:`default and project dependencies </build-default-"
"versions>`. This includes any requirements you have configured in :ref"
":`config-file/v2:requirements file`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:51
msgid ""
"If the project has extra Python requirements, :ref:`config-"
"file/v2:python.install` can be used to specify them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:56
msgid ""
"We strongly recommend :doc:`pinning all the versions </guides"
"/reproducible-builds>` required to build the documentation to avoid "
"unexpected build errors."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst
msgid "build"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:64
msgid ""
"Runs the main command to build the documentation for each of the formats "
"declared (:ref:`config-file/v2:formats`). It will use Sphinx (:ref"
":`config-file/v2:sphinx`) or MkDocs (:ref:`config-file/v2:mkdocs`) "
"depending on the project."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst
msgid "upload"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:69
msgid ""
"Once the build process finishes successfully, the resulting artifacts "
"(HTML, PDF, etc.) are uploaded to our servers. Our :doc:`CDN "
"</reference/cdn>` is then purged so your docs are *always up to date*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:75
msgid ":doc:`/build-customization`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:76
msgid ""
"Run user-defined commands if you require additional build steps or "
"customization."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:78
msgid ":doc:`/guides/build/direct-upload`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:79
msgid ""
"Build the documentation in your own continuous integration pipeline and "
"upload the result to Read the Docs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:82
msgid "Skipping builds"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:84
msgid ""
"A *skipped* build is a build that is never triggered. Using "
":doc:`automation rules </automation-rules>` with webhook filters, you can"
" tell Read the Docs to only trigger builds when something relevant has "
"changed, for example when files under ``docs/`` were modified, or when "
"the commit message doesn't contain ``[skip ci]``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:90
msgid ""
"Skipped builds don't appear in your build history and don't consume build"
" time. This is the recommended way to avoid unnecessary builds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:95
msgid ":ref:`automation-rules:Webhook filters`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:96
msgid "Filter builds by changed files, commit message, or pull request labels."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:98
msgid ":doc:`/guides/build/skip-or-cancel-builds`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:99
msgid "Examples of skipping and cancelling builds based on conditions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:102
msgid "Cancelling builds"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:104
msgid ""
"A *cancelled* build is a build that was triggered as usual, but was "
"stopped before it finished. Cancelled builds appear in your build history"
" with the *Cancelled* state. Cancelling builds allows your team to speed "
"up review times and also help us reduce server costs and our "
"environmental footprint."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:109
msgid "A couple common reasons you might want to cancel builds are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:111
msgid "the build has an external dependency that hasn't been updated"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:112
msgid ""
"your Git provider doesn't support webhook filters, and you want to stop "
"builds when there were no changes on the documentation files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:115
msgid ""
"Read the Docs supports three different mechanisms to cancel a running "
"build:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst
msgid "Manually"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:119
msgid ""
"Project administrators can go to the build detail page and click "
":guilabel:`Cancel build`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst
msgid "Automatically"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:124
msgid ""
"When Read the Docs detects a push to a version that is already building, "
"it cancels the running build and starts a new build using the latest "
"commit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst
msgid "Programmatically"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:129
msgid ""
"You can use user-defined commands on ``build.jobs`` or ``build.commands``"
" (see :doc:`build-customization`) to check for your own cancellation "
"condition and then return exit code ``183`` to cancel a build. You can "
"exit with the code ``0`` to continue running the build."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:133
msgid ""
"When this happens, Read the Docs will notify your :doc:`Git provider "
"</reference/git-integration>` the build succeeded (✅), so the pull "
"request doesn't have any failing checks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:138
msgid ""
"Take a look at the :doc:`/guides/build/skip-or-cancel-builds` page for "
"some examples."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:141
msgid "Automatic disabling of builds"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:143
msgid ""
"To reduce resource consumption and improve build queue times for all "
"users, Read the Docs will automatically disable builds for projects that "
"have too many consecutive failed builds on their default version."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:146
msgid ""
"When a project has **25 consecutive failed builds** on its default "
"version, we will disable builds for the project."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:149
msgid ""
"This helps ensure that projects with persistent build issues don't "
"consume resources that could be used by active projects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:153
msgid ""
"This only applies to the default version of a project. Builds on other "
"versions (branches, tags, pull requests) are not counted towards this "
"limit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/builds.rst:156
msgid ""
"If your project has been disabled due to consecutive build failures, "
"you'll need to re-enable from your project settings. Make sure to fix the"
" underlying issue to avoid being disabled again."
msgstr ""

